Lauren's life is full of color  she runs an eco-friendly glitter  business and loves her work  things haven't always sparkled  her old job as an air host desk left her  tired depressed and drained  this is how she felt at her lowest  before leaving last year  it got to the point where I would do a  really long day flying and every day for  about two weeks I got home and I just  sat on my bed that exhausted I couldn't  even get out of my uniform and just sat  there crying I was making myself ill  from stress from work  she says her employers failed to support  her through difficult moments like the  death of her best friend and her mom's  cancer diagnosis making long grueling  days even harder  you know when you've lost somebody you  want to go and support your family and  be there for them but you can't do that  you need to put on a smile and a brave  face and deal with hundreds of people in  a day in a metal tube in the sky where  you've got no Escape  new figures show those feelings of  dreading a job are common  the research reveals 67 of British  employees experience anxiety about the  week ahead  the top triggers are work stress at 72  percent lack of rest and sleep also at  72 percent  and their to-do list for the week ahead  at 70 percent  they also found that 53 of people feel  anxious about working in person compared  to six percent of people if they're  anxious about working from home  statistics like those are why the who  has laid out new guidance it includes  training managers to support staff and  advice on helping people come back from  mental health related absences  if you support effectively the mental  health of your Workforce you are not  losing out on productivity losses not  losing out on where people reach a point  where they have to take absence because  they're not doing as well that's really  important and also you're retaining your  staff  it's about the finer details focusing on  how every person feels at work will make  the difference  Sky News
 

